    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d271">

                <pb facs="0317" n="1151" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1151"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 657.119/374</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Schmedeman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Christiania</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-20T15:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 20, 1918—3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 5.07 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>600. Minister of Foreign Affairs informed me yesterday that Norwegian Government
                    was negotiating with Germany in regard to commodity exchange. He said that
                    Germany was asking for a written statement of commodities to be sent to Germany.
                    In reply to a question as to what compensation was expected His Excellency told
                    me it was wrought and pig iron, steel plates, and medicines. I have no
                    information about exact quantities.</p>
                <p>With regard to the American agreement, Minister for Foreign Affairs expressed the
                    hope that it would be signed by April 1 and seemed pleased at the prospect.
                    Meanwhile general opinion is somewhat depressed and pessimistic regarding
                    outcome of negotiations with us and it is not improbable that the Government
                    plans to make use of this feeling for political purposes as soon as the
                    agreement is concluded.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schmedeman</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
